# repo-doctor exceptions. One entry per line, blank lines and #-comments ignored:
#
#   <check-id> <path-glob>[::<token-glob>] reason=<text> expires=YYYY-MM-DD
#
# The check-id matches EXACTLY, never as a glob, so an entry can never widen itself into
# checks it was not written for. The path is matched as a glob. The optional ::<token-glob>
# narrows an entry to ONE finding inside a file (dead-pointer uses it) rather than switching
# the check off for the whole file. Scope every entry to the occurrence, give it a real
# reason, and delete it the moment the underlying thing is fixed.
#
# expires= is mandatory and may not sit more than 90 days out. An expired entry is itself a
# HARD violation, so a forgotten exception fails loudly instead of quietly living forever.
# A malformed line is one HARD allow-syntax line and is skipped, so a single typo cannot
# switch the scanner off for everything below it.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE IS READ FROM: the pushing checkout, never the commit being scanned. The
# pre-push hook materialises each pushed commit in a throwaway worktree and judges it with
# THIS file and THIS scripts/repo-doctor. Two consequences worth knowing before you need
# them. A commit made while the checker was weak cannot approve itself, because it is judged
# by today's rules. And an entry added in a LATER commit does clear an older commit in the
# same push range, which is the remedy when a tightened check retroactively condemns
# something an unpushed commit carried legitimately. Keep such an entry occurrence-scoped
# and short-dated, and retire it once the range is on the remote.
#
# `git push --no-verify` is the strictly worse tool: it excuses nothing and skips the scan
# of every commit in the range. An allow entry is a documented, dated, reviewable exception.
# Skipping the hook is none of those.
#
# Start empty. Stay empty for as long as you can. Example, deliberately commented out:
#
# dead-pointer knowledge/ops/os-roadmap.md::scripts/check reason=planned pass-3 work, not rot expires=2026-11-01
